WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:04.460
In this video, you learn about the Beam Search algorithm.

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.700
In the last video, you remember how for machine translation, given an input French sentence,

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.700
you don't want to output a random English translation.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.380
You want to output the best and the most likely English translation.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:21.420
The same is also true for speech recognition, where given an input audio clip, you don't

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.520
want to output a random text transcript of that audio.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.180
You want to output the best and maybe the most likely text transcript.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:32.100
Beam Search is the most widely used algorithm to do this and in this video, you see how

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.940
to get Beam Search to work for yourself.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:38.220
Let's describe Beam Search using our running example of the French sentence, jane visique

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.260
l'afrique en septemps, hopefully being translated into jane visites africa in September.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.740
The first thing Beam Search has to do is try to pick the first word of the English translation

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.180
that it's going to output.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.380
So here, I've listed all, say, 10,000 words in the vocabulary.

00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:58.140
And to simplify the problem a bit, I'm going to ignore capitalization.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.860
So I'm just listing all the words in lowercase.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:05.740
So in the first step of Beam Search, I'll use this network fragment with the encoder

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:10.860
shown in green and the decoder shown in purple to try to evaluate what is the probability

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.220
of that first word.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:17.700
So what's the probability of the first output y given the input sentence x, given the French

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.860
input?

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:25.540
So whereas 3D search will pick only the one most likely word and move on, Beam Search

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.700
instead can consider multiple alternatives.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.380
So the Beam Search algorithm has a parameter called b, which is called the beam width.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.380
And for this example, I'm going to set the beam width to be equal to 3.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.020
And what this means is Beam Search will consider not just one possibility, but consider three

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.500
at a time.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.460
So in particular, let's say evaluating this probability over different choices of the first

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:58.060
word, it finds that the choices in Jane and September are the most likely three possibilities

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.580
for the first word in the English output.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:06.780
Then Beam Search will store away in computer memory that it wants to try out all three

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.140
of these words.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:12.940
And if the beam width parameter was set differently, if the beam width parameter was, say, 10,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:17.740
then we'd keep track of not just three, but of the 10 most likely possible choices for

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.140
the first word.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:24.260
So to be clear, in order to perform this first step of Beam Search, what you need to

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.660
do is run the input French sentence through this encoder network.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:34.860
And then this first step of the decoder network, if this is a softmax output over all 10,000

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:42.820
possibilities, then you would take those 10,000 possible outputs and keep in memory

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.020
which were the top three.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.660
Let's go on to the second step of Beam Search.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:53.980
Having picked in Jane and September as the three most likely choices of the first word,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.260
what Beam Search will do now is for each of these three choices, consider what should

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.340
be the second word.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.020
So after in, maybe the second word is A, or maybe it's Aaron.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:06.980
I'm just listing words from the vocabulary or from the dictionary.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.100
Or somewhere down the list will be September.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.220
Somewhere down the list is visit and then all the way to Z.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.740
Maybe the last word is Zulu.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.820
So to evaluate the probability of the second word, it will use this neural network fragment

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:24.540
where there's the encoder in green.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:29.260
And for the decoder portion, when trying to decide what comes after in, remember the

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:33.300
decoder first outputs Y hat 1.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:39.060
So we're going to set this Y hat 1 to the word in and feed this back in.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:45.660
So this is the word in because we decided for now that because we're trying to figure

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.980
out that the first word was in, what is the second word?

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:53.780
And then this will output, I guess, Y hat 2.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:04:02.820
And so by hard wiring Y hat 1 here, really the input here to be the first word in, this

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:09.860
network fragment can be used to evaluate what is the probability of the second word given

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:15.540
the input French sentence and that the first word of the translation has been the word

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.540
in.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.620
Now notice that what we ultimately care about in this second step of beam search is to

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.140
define the pair of the first and second words that is most likely.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.620
So not just the second word is most likely, but the pair of the first and second words

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.100
are most likely.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:38.260
And by the rules of conditional probability, this can be expressed as P of the first word

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:49.300
times P of probability of the second word, which you are getting from this neural network

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.100
fragment.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:56.740
And so if for each of the three words you've chosen in Jane and September, you've saved

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.900
away this probability, then you can multiply them by this second probability to get the

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:05.380
probability of the first and second words.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:10.220
So now you've seen how if the first word was in, how you can evaluate the probability of

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:11.700
the second word.

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.580
Now if the first word was Jane, you do the same thing.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:25.700
The sentence could be Jane A, Jane Erin, and so on down to Jane is, Jane visits, and so

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.060
on.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:35.310
And you would use this neural network fragment, and let me draw this in as well, where here

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:39.870
you would hardwire Y hat 1 to be Jane.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:51.310
And so with the first word Y1 hat hardwired as Jane, then this neural network fragment can

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:55.590
tell you what's the probability of the second word given the input X and given that the

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:57.950
first word was Jane.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:06.750
And then same as above, you can multiply with P of Y1 to get the probability of Y1

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:14.590
and Y2 for each of these 10,000 different possible choices for the second word.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:18.910
And then finally, you do the same thing for September.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:25.860
All the words from A down to Zulu and use this network fragment, I just draw this

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:32.980
in as well, to see if the first word was September, what are the most likely options

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:35.940
for the second word.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:41.540
So for this second step of beam search, because we're continuing to use a beam

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:46.900
width of 3, and because there are 10,000 words in the vocabulary, you'd end up

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:52.980
considering 3 times 10,000 or 30,000 possibilities because there are 10,000

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.540
here, 10,000 here, 10,000 here.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:07:01.540
So it's a beam width times the number of words in the vocabulary.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:06.500
And what you do is you evaluate all of these 30,000 options according to the

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:10.500
probability of the first and second word, and then pick the top three.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:15.220
So with a cut down, these 30,000 possibilities down to three again, down

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:17.140
to the beam width around again.

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:22.900
And so let's say that these 30,000 choices, the most likely were in September

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:30.180
and say Jane is and Jane visits.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:34.580
Sorry, this is a bit messy, but if those are the most likely three out of the 30,000

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:41.140
choices, then that's what beam search would memorize away and take on to the

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:43.940
next step of beam search.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:48.900
So notice one thing, if beam search decides that the three most likely

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:54.500
choices for the first and second words are in September or Jane is or Jane

00:07:54.500 --> 00:08:00.020
visits, then what that means is that it is now rejecting September as a

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:06.660
candidate for the first word of the output English translation.

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.420
So we're now down to two possibilities for the first word,

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:14.820
but we still have a beam width of three keeping track of

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:21.060
three choices for pairs of Y1, Y2.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.820
Before going on to the third step of beam search, just want to notice that

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:28.020
because the beam width is equal to three, at every step

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:34.820
you instantiate three copies of the network to evaluate these partial

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:40.420
sentence fragments of the output. And it's because the beam width is equal to

00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:43.700
three that you have three copies of the network

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:47.060
with different choices for the first word.

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:51.620
But these three copies of the network can be very efficiently used to evaluate

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:58.900
all 30,000 options for the second word. So just don't instantiate 30,000 copies

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:02.260
of the network. You need only three copies of the network

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:06.500
to very quickly evaluate all 10,000 possible outputs

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:11.860
at that softmax output, say for Y2. Let's just quickly illustrate one more

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:15.860
step of beam search. So we said that the most likely

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:20.180
choices for the first two words were in September, Jane is and Jane visits.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:23.220
And for each of these pairs of words, we should have saved away in computer

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:27.140
memory the probability of Y1 and Y2

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:30.100
given the input X, given the French sentence

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:36.020
X. So similar to before, we now want to consider what is the third word. So is it

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.940
in September A, in September Aaron, all the way down to

00:09:39.940 --> 00:09:45.060
is in September Zulu. And to evaluate possible choices for the

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:49.220
third word, you use this network fragments where you hardwire

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:53.300
the first word here to be in, the second word to be September.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:57.060
And so this network fragment allows you to evaluate what's the probability of the

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.500
third word, given the input French sentence X, and

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:08.560
given that the first two words are in September in the English output. And

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:12.560
then you do the same thing for the second

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:22.700
fragment, so like so, and same thing for Jane visits.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:26.860
And so beam search will then once again pick the top three possibilities, maybe it

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:32.220
thinks in September Jane is a likely outcome or Jane

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:38.300
is visiting is likely or maybe Jane visits.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:41.660
Africa is likely for the first three words

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:45.500
and then it keeps going. And then you go on to the fourth step of beam search and

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:49.420
add one more word and on it goes. And the outcome of this

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:53.100
process hopefully will be that adding one word at a time, that beam

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:56.300
search will decide that Jane visits Africa in September

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:59.820
will be terminated by the end of sentence symbol and then using that in

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:04.060
the system, which is quite common, that will find that this is a likely

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.060
output English sentence. And you see more details of this for

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:11.580
yourself in this week's programming exercise as well, where you get to play

00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:17.920
with beam search yourself. So with a beam with a three beam search

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.960
considers three possibilities at a time. Notice that if the beam

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:26.320
width was set to be equal to one, so it considers only one,

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:30.560
then this essentially becomes the greedy search algorithm,

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:34.960
which we had discussed in the last video. But by considering multiple

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:38.960
possibilities, say three or ten or some of the number at the same time,

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:43.040
beam search will usually find a much better output sentence than greedy

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.600
search. You've now seen how beam search works, but it turns out there's some

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.640
additional tips and tricks from refinements that helps you to make beam

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:54.160
search work even better. Let's go on to the next video to take a look.
